  (uuid_generate_v4(), 'kingman-mine-campitos-turquoise-juniper-seed-bead-stretch-bracelet-ghost-bead', '4583810473', 'https://www.etsy.com/listing/4583810473/kingman-mine-campitos-turquoise-juniper?click_key=Euvi7hhn0ZWXj9DR2e8OLySZkf1f%3ALT0ca43b006e920efabc9f478afc95516b14e48cea&click_sum=bf62cf8e&ls=a&ga_order=most_relevant&ga_search_type=all&ga_view_type=gallery&ga_search_query=Navajo+jewelry&ref=search_grid-89343-10-7&sr_prefetch=1&pf_from=search&pro=1&sts=1',
   'Kingman Mine, Campitos Turquoise & Juniper Seed Bead Stretch Bracelet ~ Ghost Bead', 'Genuine Kingman Mine turquoise spheres (United States origin) and Campitos turquoise tube beads (Mexico origin) with traditionally harvested Juniper seed beads on super strong stretch Powercord. 3 sizes to choose from! Listing is for one (1) bracelet. Ghost beads are a traditional adornment among the Navajo people of the Southwestern United States. They are made from dried juniper or cedar berries that are usually lightly smoked and strung in a classic manner. Often, they will include glass beads intertwined with the berries if they’re in short supply. The berries are only collected after they have fallen to the ground and have been eaten by ants. The ants go in one end of the berry and devour the insides, leaving a hollow dried skin. An artisan will make a hole in the other end to complete the bead’s creation. The jewelry is said to bring peace, harmony and safety to the wearer. Many Navajos will keep these beads beside them when they sleep since these beads are traditionally believed to ward off nightmares caused by evil spirits. They are also believed to ward away negative energy throughout the day. The name “ghost beads” carries a deeper significance rooted in Navajo culture and spirituality. These beads are not simply adorned for their aesthetic appeal; they are called “ghost beads” due to their profound connection to the spiritual realm. In Navajo belief, the term “ghost” encompasses more than just departed souls; it encompasses the presence of spirits, both benevolent and malevolent, that coexist with the living. Ghost beads are named so because they are believed to possess the power to protect individuals from negative spirits and malevolent energies. When worn or kept nearby, they act as a shield, preventing these unseen forces from causing harm. The choice of juniper berries is significant as juniper is considered a purifying and protective element in Navajo culture. Just as juniper ashes were used to ward off darkness and curses in the past, ghost beads m', 'turquoise-jewelry',
